titre$ Key to the European species of the genus Resupinatus
com$ Adapt G Fannechere 30 nov 2018
rem$ Clé utilisable via le logiciel Mycoclé : http://mycolim.free.fr/DOC_SML/mycoCLE/Charg_Mycocle.htm

1,0$source : "The Genera Hohenbuehelia and Resupinatus in Europe" ///Giovanni Consiglio(Author), Ledo Setti(Author)/// 2017 Monografie di Pagine di Micologia Volume: 3
1,0$

1,0 $ Spores ellipsoid, on average 6.8 x 4.2 µm  (Vm = 63  µm3), on the ground or on needles litter in coniferous woods $ Resupinatus niger
1,2 $ Cheilometuloids absent
2,3 $ Spores globose to subglobose
2,7 $ Spores oblong
3,0 $ Basidiomes cup-shaped, growing on a common subiculum, as groups of300-1000 individuals.Hymenophore not gilled. Spores on average 5.6 x 5.1 µm (Vm = 77 µm 3) $ Resupinatus poriaeformis
3,4 $ Basidiomes not growing on a common subiculum, groups little numerous. Hymenophore gilled
4,0 $ Spores very small, on average 4.8 x 4.2  µm  (Vm = 45 (µm3), pileus blackish brown, completelycoverd with a greyish down $ Resupinatus rouxii
4,5 $ Spores larger, pileus at first white, grey to brownish grey

5,0 $  Pileus white, spores on average 6.0 x 5.2 µm  (V = 89  µm3) $Resupinatus hausknechtii
5,6 $  Pileus at first grey to brownish grey, spores on average 5.1 x 4.7  µm (Vm = 60  µm3) 6
6,0 $  Pileus cup-shaped then spathulate to fan-shaped, pale grey, brownish grey then blackish grey, covered with a coarse greyish to brownish grey tomentum$ Resupinatus applicatus  
6,0 ,n$The most evident macroscopic feature is the colour of the mycelial felt that in this psecies can be whitish, greyish to brownish grey, never black.
6,0 $ Pileus cup-shaped, greyish to brownish grey then blackish grey, covered with a blackish tomentum $ Resupinatus trichotis
6,0 ,n$  R. trichotis presents a prominent central disk, coverd with thick-walled black hairs.
7,8 $ Basidiomes small sized, cyphelloid or small cup-shaped
7,11 $ Basidiomes larger
8,0 $  Basidiomes lying over a subiculum, in groups of 10-150 individuals. Pileus 0.2-0.4 mmbroad. Spores oblong, on average 7.8 x 3.8  µm (V =59  µm3)$Resupinatus conspersus
8,9 $ Basidiomes not lying over a subiculum, solitary or in small groups
9,0 $ Hymenophore smooth. Pileus0.5-1.5 mm broad. Spores broadly ellipsoid, on average 5.5 x 4.8 µm (V = 66  µm3) $ Resupinatus griseopallidus
9,10 $ Hymenophore at least belatedly pliciform or gilled. Pileus 0.5-3 mm broad

10,0 $ Pileus only belatedly striate. Lamellae absent in the early stages of growth, then imperfectly formed to pliciform. Spores broadly ellipsoid, on average 5.2 x 4.2  µm (V = 49 urn3) $ Resupinatus kavinae

10,0 ,n$ R. kavinae is strongly characterized, when young, by its pileus not striate and by its smooth hymenophore. Its suprapellis has an ixocutis structure.
10,0 $ Pileus strongly striate from the beginning. Lamellae distant, at first little numerous. Spores broadly ellipsoid, on average 5.2 x 4.6  µm (Vm = 63  µm3) $ Resupinatus striatulus

10,0,n$R. striatulus is distinguished by its pileus strongly striate and completely covered with a thin whitish to greyish frost and by its suprapellis with an ixotrichoderm structure.
11,0 $ Pileus 20-60 mm broad, at first cup-shaped then spathulate to fan-shaped, spores oblong, on average 7.8 x 4.3  µm (Vm = 77  µm3) $ Resupinatus vetlinianus
11,0 $ Pileus 1-9 mm broad, cup- or kidney-shaped. Spores allantoid, on average 6.5 x 3.2  µm (V = 36  µm3) $ Resupinatus europaeus

